pub fn copy -> CorrectionCopy = CorrectionCopy {
  summary: "Construct an Iterator from a function that computes an element and next state. Returning Nothing from step ends the sequence.",
  taskTitle: "Generate a sequence with a stopping condition",
  purpose: "When a function can determine the next value from a remaining count, unfold can build the sequence. This example decreases its state from 3 to zero, yielding 3, 2, 1 before it ends.",
  setup: "The Maybe<(Int, Int)> result of fn countdown means either Just containing the current element and next state, or Nothing for the end. The if returns Just only while remaining > 0. iterator.unfold countdown 3 passes that function and the initial state 3.",
  after: [
    "[value | value <- ...] reads an Iterator in order into an Array. Starting at 3 gives 3, 2, 1; starting at zero gives an empty Array. Calling unfold alone does not execute step; reading requests values and executes it.",
    "repeatValue returns Just (value, value): it yields the value and keeps the same state, so its Iterator repeats that value without ending. The example calls next only once and displays value from Just (value, _); _ discards the unused continuation. It obtains the first 10 without collecting the entire sequence.",
    "import * as iterator gives the operations a prefix. The do in pub effect fn main runs println in order, and show converts results to text. match branches on the Just and Nothing forms."
  ],
  comparisonBefore: "TypeScript generator functions can produce the same sequences. A function* body progresses when requested and pauses at yield after supplying an element. Only the finite countdown is collected with Array.from; repeatValue receives one next call.",
  comparisonAfter: "The displayed sequences and first value 10 agree. A JavaScript generator advances its internal execution position. Seseragi passes state explicitly to step and returns a remaining Iterator from next.",
  details: [
    "In unfold<S, A>, S is the state type and A is the yielded element type; they need not be the same. step takes an S and returns Just (an A, the next S) or Nothing. Both types happen to be Int in this example.",
    "step is pure. It is not called during unfold and is called once for each next. Re-reading the same Iterator gives the same result, but caching is not guaranteed. Use the relevant Effect or Stream APIs for I/O or asynchronous processing.",
    "Collecting an endless Iterator, or reading it to completion in an effectful for, will not terminate. Read only a bounded number of next steps or include a Nothing stopping condition as countdown does."
